<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of St Matthew's Day</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1217">1217</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-crusade">Livonian Crusade</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of St Matthew's Day was fought on 21 September 1217 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viljandi">Viljandi</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-crusade">Livonian Crusade</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a> and their Liv and Latgalian allies defeated an Estonian army. The Estonian leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lembitu">Lembitu</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sakala</a> and the Liv chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caupo">Caupo of Turaida</a> were both killed, and Sakala again submitted to the crusaders.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By 1217 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a>, based in Riga, with Bishop Albert and their Latgalian and Livonian allies, had forced the elders of Ugandi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sakala</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarva-county">Järvamaa</a> and Soontagana to recognise their authority. The princes of the Novgorod Republic and the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a>, who regarded Ugandi as part of their sphere, then took the stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otepaa">Otepää</a> after a long siege, and the forces of the order and the bishop had to withdraw from Ugandi. In August the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> sent gifts to the Russians and asked them to join a new campaign, and Svjatoslav Mstislavich, the new prince of Novgorod, agreed.</p><p>At the call of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lembitu">Lembitu</a>, elder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sakala</a>, about 6,000 men from Sakala, Läänemaa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harju-county">Harjumaa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarva-county">Järvamaa</a>, Revala and Virumaa gathered by the Navesti river around 6 September; of the larger counties only Ugandi and Saaremaa stayed away. They waited 15 days, but the promised Novgorodian troops never came. Riga quickly raised an army of 3,000, probably led by the Master of the Order, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volquin">Volkwin</a>, with Count Albert of Lauenburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caupo">Caupo</a> among its leaders.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The army camped near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viljandi">Viljandi</a> castle on the evening before St Matthew's Day and next day pursued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> until they came out of the woods ready for battle, probably in the area between Vanamõisa, Karuse and the former Madi farm. The Germans in the centre advanced slowly in formation and broke the Estonian centre, the largest division. The Sakalians on the Estonian right had held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgalians">Latgalians</a>, but fled when the centre collapsed, and in the rout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lembitu">Lembitu</a> and the leaders Vottele, Manivalde and Vytamas were killed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livonians</a> on the other flank at first fell back under a hail of spears, then joined the pursuit.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-livonia">Henry of Livonia</a> counts about 1,000 Estonian dead and 2,000 horses taken, the Livonian Rhymed Chronicle 1,400 dead. The victors camped for three days at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lembitu">Lembitu</a>'s village, pillaging the area, until his brother Unnepewe and other surviving elders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sakala</a> renewed peace and gave hostages. Many scholars regard the battle as effectively the final battle of the Ancient Estonian War for Freedom, while Enn Tarvel, Artur Vassar and Sulev Vahtre see a heavy blow rather than a decisive turn. A Danish tradition recorded by Petrus Olai in 1527 tells of a flag descending from the sky during a battle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viljandi">Viljandi</a>, and some historians have linked it with this battle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of St. Matthew's Day” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lembitu was killed at the <b>Battle of St Matthew's Day</b> near Viljandi in 1217, after which Sakala again submitted to the crusaders.</blockquote><a href="/state/sakala">Sakala</a></li><li><blockquote>Caupo was killed at the <b>Battle of St Matthew's Day</b> in 1217.</blockquote><a href="/state/turaida">Turaida</a></li></ul></section>
